"""Fifty additional canonical works. All summaries are original learning adaptations."""
def register(add):
    def A(title,author,year,era,genre,setting,hook,cast,frames,q,answer,src,note='',date=None):
        url=f'https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/{src}' if isinstance(src,int) else src
        add(title,author,year,date or str(year),era,genre,setting,hook,cast,frames,q,answer,source=url,note=note)

    A("Utopia","Thomas More",1516,"renaissance","Humanist dialogue","Europe and an imagined island","An imaginary island makes familiar injustice look strange.","Raphael Hythloday=Traveller describing Utopia|More=Character debating the traveller|Peter Giles=Introduces the conversation", '''
A traveller arrives~More and Peter Giles meet Hythloday, whose voyages provide a different view of European society. His authority comes from travel, but his claims invite debate rather than automatic acceptance.~Raphael Hythloday,More,Peter Giles~road~A STRANGER questions the familiar
Counsel or compromise~The men debate whether a philosopher should advise kings. More's character favours practical influence; Hythloday thinks corrupt courts distort advice and make meaningful reform almost impossible.~Raphael Hythloday,More~crown~CAN wisdom serve power?
The punishment problem~Hythloday attacks harsh penalties for theft. Enclosure and poverty help produce the behaviour that governments then punish, moving attention from individual blame toward social arrangements.~Raphael Hythloday~chains~ASK what creates crime
An island without private property~Utopia organises work, education and distribution around shared resources. The description challenges European assumptions about wealth, leisure and the relationship between property and conflict.~Raphael Hythloday~village~PROPERTY becomes a question
Order has its costs~The island also regulates travel and households and uses slavery. Its apparent harmony is therefore unsettling: an attractive arrangement can still rely on restrictions and unequal power.~Raphael Hythloday,More~gate~PERFECTION contains coercion
Belief and public life~Religious practices differ, with significant toleration but important exclusions. The island's treatment of war, worship and civic duty extends the experiment beyond a simple economic blueprint.~Raphael Hythloday~temple~TOLERATION has limits
The debate remains open~More's character admires some arrangements but rejects others. The dialogue ends without converting every detail into an endorsed programme, leaving readers to test the distance between ideals and institutions.~More,Raphael Hythloday~book~AN EXPERIMENT not a verdict''',"Why is Utopia not simply a blueprint?","Its dialogue, irony and troubling institutions leave the imagined society open to criticism.",2130,"Originally written in Latin. More is a character as well as the author; the two should not be treated as interchangeable.")
    A("Romeo and Juliet","William Shakespeare",1595,"renaissance","Tragedy","Verona and Mantua","A private love cannot escape a public feud.","Romeo=Montague lover|Juliet=Capulet lover|Mercutio=Romeo's witty friend|Tybalt=Juliet's combative cousin|Friar Lawrence=Arranges the secret marriage", '''
The city belongs to the feud~Street fighting reveals the hatred between Montagues and Capulets. Romeo, initially absorbed in Rosaline, has not yet met the person who will transform his life.~Romeo,Tybalt~sword~HATRED sets the stage
The masked encounter~Romeo enters a Capulet celebration and meets Juliet. Their attraction precedes recognition of their family names, turning immediate wonder into a dangerous allegiance.~Romeo,Juliet~mask~LOVE crosses a boundary
A promise at the balcony~Juliet questions the power of names while Romeo promises devotion. With the Nurse's help and Friar Lawrence's hopes for reconciliation, the lovers marry secretly.~Romeo,Juliet,Friar Lawrence~heart~PRIVATE vows public danger
The duel changes everything~Romeo tries to avoid fighting his new relative Tybalt. Mercutio is killed, Romeo kills Tybalt in anger, and banishment separates the newly married couple.~Romeo,Mercutio,Tybalt~sword~ANGER destroys the plan
Juliet chooses the potion~Pressed to marry Paris, Juliet accepts the Friar's sleeping potion. The plan requires a message explaining that her apparent death is temporary, making accurate communication crucial.~Juliet,Friar Lawrence~laboratory~A PLAN depends on a letter
The message fails~Romeo hears that Juliet is dead before the explanatory letter reaches him. He buys poison and rushes to the tomb, acting on incomplete knowledge rather than waiting.~Romeo,Juliet~letter~NEWS without understanding
The tomb and the families~Romeo dies beside Juliet; she wakes and kills herself after finding him dead. Their families reconcile in grief, gaining understanding only after the young lives are lost.~Romeo,Juliet,Friar Lawrence~grave~RECONCILIATION arrives too late''',"What makes the potion plan fail?","The explanatory message does not reach Romeo before he hears the false report of Juliet's death.",1513,"Approximate composition date.",date="c. 1595")
    A("A Midsummer Night’s Dream","William Shakespeare",1595,"renaissance","Comedy","Athens and an enchanted wood","Love loses its bearings in a forest of transformations.","Hermia=Loves Lysander|Lysander=Hermia's chosen partner|Helena=Loves Demetrius|Demetrius=Pursues Hermia|Puck=Mischievous spirit|Titania=Fairy queen|Bottom=Amateur actor", '''
A father's demand~Hermia is ordered to accept Demetrius, despite loving Lysander. She and Lysander plan to flee Athens, while Helena reveals the plan hoping to win Demetrius's attention.~Hermia,Lysander,Helena~gate~DESIRE defies authority
The fairies quarrel~Oberon and Titania dispute possession of an Indian boy. Oberon orders a magical flower used on Titania and asks Puck to help an unhappy Athenian lover.~Titania,Puck~garden~MAGIC serves a quarrel
The wrong eyelids~Puck mistakes Lysander for Demetrius. The potion makes Lysander pursue Helena, and further interference leaves both men chasing her while Hermia feels abandoned.~Puck,Lysander,Helena~mirror~LOVE becomes unstable
Bottom transformed~Workmen rehearse a play in the wood. Puck gives Bottom an ass's head, and enchanted Titania falls in love with him, joining fairy grandeur to ordinary theatrical comedy.~Bottom,Titania,Puck~mask~HIGH meets humble
Friendship is wounded~Helena believes the others mock her; Hermia cannot understand the sudden rejection. The emotional confusion threatens not only romance but the women's earlier friendship.~Helena,Hermia,Demetrius~storm~MISREADING hurts friendship
The forest is repaired~Oberon releases Titania, and Puck restores Lysander's love for Hermia. Demetrius remains enchanted toward Helena; the apparent harmony therefore retains an uneasy magical dependency.~Puck,Hermia,Lysander~forest~ORDER with a complication
The wedding performance~The couples celebrate with the workmen's comically awkward Pyramus and Thisbe. Puck's closing address asks the audience to treat the play as a dream if it has offended.~Bottom,Puck~stage~THEATRE turns error into delight''',"Which lover remains under the potion's influence at the end?","Demetrius, whose restored love for Helena remains magically induced.",1514,"Approximate composition date. The ending restores social order but does not remove every question about consent.",date="c. 1595–1596")
    A("Hamlet","William Shakespeare",1601,"renaissance","Revenge tragedy","Elsinore in Denmark","A demand for revenge becomes a crisis of knowledge.","Hamlet=Prince seeking truth|Claudius=King and Hamlet's uncle|Gertrude=Hamlet's mother|Ophelia=Woman caught between loyalties|Horatio=Trusted friend|Laertes=Ophelia's brother", '''
The ghost's accusation~Hamlet mourns his father and resents Gertrude's marriage to Claudius. The dead king's ghost accuses Claudius of murder and demands revenge, but a supernatural claim still needs testing.~Hamlet,Claudius,Gertrude~ghost~REVENGE begins with uncertainty
A role of madness~Hamlet adopts strange behaviour to investigate the court. Ophelia and her father Polonius become entangled in surveillance, while Hamlet's distrust damages relationships that might otherwise support him.~Hamlet,Ophelia~mask~PERFORMANCE obscures truth
The play catches the king~Visiting actors stage a murder resembling the ghost's account. Claudius's disturbed reaction strengthens Hamlet's conviction, turning theatrical invention into evidence about political reality.~Hamlet,Claudius,Horatio~stage~FICTION tests a fact
The wrong man behind the curtain~Confronting Gertrude, Hamlet stabs the hidden Polonius. His attempt to expose one crime creates another death, driving Ophelia toward breakdown and Laertes toward revenge.~Hamlet,Gertrude,Ophelia~dagger~REVENGE harms bystanders
A voyage and a funeral~Hamlet escapes a plot to kill him abroad and returns to Denmark. Ophelia has drowned; in the graveyard, skulls make mortality concrete and grief erupts at her burial.~Hamlet,Laertes,Horatio~grave~DEATH exceeds the argument
The poisoned match~Claudius and Laertes arrange a duel using poison. Gertrude drinks the poisoned cup; wounds expose the scheme, and Hamlet finally kills Claudius.~Hamlet,Claudius,Laertes~sword~THE TRAP catches its makers
Horatio must tell the story~Hamlet dies after asking Horatio to explain what happened. Fortinbras arrives to assume authority, leaving the ending concerned with both political succession and the fragile preservation of truth.~Hamlet,Horatio~book~WITNESS survives revenge''',"Why does Hamlet stage the play?","To test the ghost's accusation by watching Claudius respond to a represented murder.",1524,"Approximate composition date; quartos and the Folio differ.",date="c. 1600–1601")
    A("King Lear","William Shakespeare",1606,"renaissance","Tragedy","Legendary Britain","A king mistakes flattery for love and learns through loss.","Lear=King surrendering his kingdom|Cordelia=Honest youngest daughter|Goneril=Eldest daughter|Regan=Second daughter|Gloucester=Father deceived by his son|Edmund=Ambitious illegitimate son|Edgar=Gloucester's loyal son", '''
The love test~Lear divides his kingdom according to declarations of affection. Goneril and Regan flatter him; Cordelia refuses exaggeration, so Lear disinherits her and banishes the loyal Kent.~Lear,Cordelia,Goneril~crown~WORDS mistaken for love
Authority without protection~Lear expects royal treatment after surrendering power. His older daughters restrict his retinue, revealing that ceremonial status cannot protect him from the consequences of his own settlement.~Lear,Goneril,Regan~gate~POWER given away
Another father deceived~Edmund fabricates evidence against Edgar and wins Gloucester's trust. The parallel plot repeats Lear's error: a parent judges appearances badly and drives away the faithful child.~Gloucester,Edmund,Edgar~letter~FALSE evidence wins trust
The storm strips the king~Out on the heath with the Fool and disguised Edgar, Lear confronts exposure and suffering. His vulnerability opens sympathy for people whose needs he overlooked as king.~Lear,Edgar~storm~SUFFERING teaches attention
Blindness brings recognition~Gloucester is blinded after helping Lear. He learns Edmund's betrayal and encounters Edgar in disguise, reversing the difference between physical sight and moral understanding.~Gloucester,Edgar,Edmund~mirror~BLINDNESS uncovers truth
A fragile reunion~Cordelia returns with forces to help her father, and Lear accepts her love without demanding a performance. Their reconciliation is real but cannot guarantee political victory.~Lear,Cordelia~heart~FORGIVENESS without safety
Loss beyond repair~Defeat places Lear and Cordelia in captivity. Cordelia is hanged on Edmund's orders, and Lear dies grieving over her body; restored knowledge cannot restore the dead.~Lear,Cordelia,Edgar~grave~KNOWING comes too late''',"What connects the Lear and Gloucester plots?","Both fathers misjudge their children, reject loyalty and learn the truth through suffering.",1532,"Approximate composition date. The tragedy survives in materially different quarto and Folio versions.",date="c. 1605–1606")
    A("Death Be Not Proud","John Donne",1633,"renaissance","Metaphysical sonnet","An imagined address to Death","The speaker cross-examines Death and overturns its claim to power.","The speaker=Challenges Death through faith|Death=Personified opponent", '''
An unexpected challenge~Instead of trembling before Death, the speaker addresses it directly and denies its reputation for overwhelming power. The sonnet begins like an argument against a boastful opponent.~The speaker,Death~crown~DEATH is challenged
The claim of victory questioned~Death appears to overthrow people, but the speaker insists it cannot finally destroy them. Religious belief changes the meaning of what ordinarily looks like an absolute ending.~The speaker,Death~grave~APPEARANCE is not final reality
Sleep becomes the analogy~Rest and sleep bring pleasure, so the speaker imagines death as another form of release. The argument turns a frightening image into a familiar and temporary experience.~The speaker~bed~SLEEP reduces terror
Death has masters~Fate, chance, rulers and desperate people govern Death's opportunities. Its supposed sovereignty collapses when the speaker presents it as dependent upon forces outside itself.~The speaker,Death~chains~THE RULER is a servant
Its company is degraded~Death lives beside poison, war and sickness. Even drugs and charms can produce sleep, further undermining its claim to a unique and magnificent power.~Death~laboratory~ITS POWER is ordinary
One waking changes everything~The speaker imagines a short sleep followed by eternal waking. The Christian resurrection provides the decisive premise: bodily death cannot defeat everlasting life.~The speaker~gate~WAKING defeats finality
The final reversal~The ending makes Death itself the thing that dies. The sonnet's wit serves faith by converting a universal fear into a paradoxical declaration of confidence.~The speaker,Death~stars~DEATH loses the last word''',"What belief supports the final paradox?","Resurrection and eternal life make bodily death temporary rather than ultimate.", "https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poems/44107/holy-sonnets-death-be-not-proud","Probably composed around 1609; published posthumously in 1633. These are argumentative steps, not a narrated encounter.",date="1633; composed c. 1609")
    A("The Duchess of Malfi","John Webster",1614,"renaissance","Tragedy","Malfi and surrounding Italian courts","A woman's private choice exposes the violence of inherited power.","The Duchess=Widow choosing to remarry|Antonio=Her steward and secret husband|Ferdinand=Her controlling brother|The Cardinal=Her other brother|Bosola=Spy and conflicted instrument", '''
A forbidden remarriage~The Duchess's brothers forbid her to marry again. She nevertheless chooses Antonio, a man beneath her social rank, asserting a personal freedom their family power refuses to recognise.~The Duchess,Antonio,Ferdinand~heart~CHOICE challenges rank
The household watched~Bosola enters her service as a spy. Signs of pregnancy and the birth of children make the private marriage increasingly vulnerable to the surveillance surrounding her.~The Duchess,Bosola~mirror~PRIVACY under inspection
The secret household breaks~When danger grows, the Duchess helps Antonio escape under a misleading accusation. Separation protects him temporarily but leaves her exposed to her brothers' anger.~The Duchess,Antonio~gate~LOVE seeks a way out
Trust betrayed~Bosola wins information about Antonio and the children, then reports it. His intelligence and uneasy conscience do not prevent him from serving the machinery of persecution.~Bosola,The Duchess,Ferdinand~letter~KNOWLEDGE becomes a weapon
The prison theatre~The Duchess suffers staged horrors, imprisonment and psychological torment. She maintains her identity and dignity even as Ferdinand tries to reduce her to terror and obedience.~The Duchess,Ferdinand~stage~DIGNITY under coercion
The killing and its aftermath~The Duchess and members of her household are murdered. Ferdinand becomes mentally disordered, while Bosola recognises the horror of his own complicity and turns against his employers.~The Duchess,Bosola,Ferdinand~grave~THE SERVANT confronts guilt
Revenge deepens the ruin~Bosola accidentally kills Antonio before the final violence consumes the brothers and himself. A surviving child offers succession, but the price of the Duchess's autonomy remains devastating.~Bosola,Antonio,The Cardinal~sword~REPAIR cannot erase violence''',"Why does the secret marriage provoke the brothers?","It defies their control over lineage, status and the Duchess's personal life.",2232,"First performed around 1613–1614; first printed 1623.",date="c. 1613–1614")
    A("The Pilgrim’s Progress","John Bunyan",1678,"restoration","Religious allegory","A dream landscape of spiritual trials","A burdened traveller learns that the journey is the lesson.","Christian=Pilgrim leaving destruction|Evangelist=Points out the way|Faithful=Companion who dies for belief|Hopeful=Companion who continues|Christiana=Christian's wife and later pilgrim", '''
A book and a burden~Christian reads a warning about destruction and feels the weight of his sin. Evangelist directs him toward a gate, beginning a journey his neighbours consider foolish.~Christian,Evangelist~book~READING awakens the need
The first wrong turns~The Slough of Despond and misleading advice threaten his progress. These obstacles translate doubt and easy moral shortcuts into places that can trap an uncertain traveller.~Christian~road~SHORTCUTS delay the journey
The burden falls~After passing the gate and receiving instruction, Christian reaches the cross. His burden falls away, but release from guilt does not mean that future trials disappear.~Christian~gate~RELEASE is a beginning
Enemies and companionship~Christian confronts Apollyon and the Valley of the Shadow of Death. Faithful's companionship offers support, showing that spiritual courage can include both solitary struggle and shared endurance.~Christian,Faithful~dragon~COURAGE needs perseverance
Vanity Fair~The pilgrims reject a marketplace's values and face persecution. Faithful is executed; Hopeful joins Christian, carrying forward the influence of the witness who has died.~Christian,Faithful,Hopeful~coins~VALUES bring conflict
Doubting Castle~A mistaken route leads to captivity under Giant Despair. Remembering the key of Promise lets the companions escape, making recollection a practical defence against hopelessness.~Christian,Hopeful~castle~REMEMBER the promise
Across the final river~Christian and Hopeful cross the river of death and reach the Celestial City. Part Two later follows Christiana and the children, widening pilgrimage beyond the first traveller.~Christian,Hopeful,Christiana~river~THE JOURNEY can be shared''',"What allows escape from Doubting Castle?","Christian remembers the key of Promise, which opens the prison doors.",131,"Part One appeared in 1678; Part Two in 1684. This overview centres on Part One.",date="1678 and 1684")
    A("Robinson Crusoe","Daniel Defoe",1719,"restoration","Adventure novel","An Atlantic island and colonial trade routes","Survival becomes a story about labour, providence and power.","Crusoe=Shipwrecked narrator|Friday=Man rescued and subordinated by Crusoe|The captain=Helped during the escape from the island", '''
The forbidden voyage~Crusoe ignores his father's advice to seek a secure life. Trade, captivity and plantation ownership draw him into an Atlantic world shaped by commerce and coercion.~Crusoe~ship~RESTLESSNESS sets sail
Shipwreck and salvage~A voyage to acquire enslaved people ends in disaster. Alone on an island, Crusoe salvages tools and supplies from the wreck, turning immediate panic into the problem of staying alive.~Crusoe~sea~RESOURCES make survival possible
A world built by labour~He constructs shelter, grows grain and domesticates goats. Journals and inventories organise experience, while repeated failures teach him practical patience rather than instant mastery.~Crusoe~home~LABOUR creates order
An inward reckoning~Illness and isolation lead Crusoe toward prayer and a providential reading of his life. His religious interpretation accompanies, rather than replaces, the material work of survival.~Crusoe~book~ISOLATION changes belief
The footprint~A footprint shatters his sense of secure possession. Fear of visitors makes the island feel unfamiliar again, revealing how fragile his apparent control has always been.~Crusoe~road~ONE MARK changes the world
Friday and the hierarchy~Crusoe rescues Friday and gains companionship. Yet he names, instructs and commands him, so the relationship combines affection and assistance with colonial domination.~Crusoe,Friday~chains~COMPANIONSHIP is unequal
Rescue and return~Helping a captain recover his ship allows Crusoe to leave after many years. Wealth awaits him, but the narrative's achievements remain entwined with the exploitative systems that enabled them.~Crusoe,Friday,The captain~ship~SURVIVAL within empire''',"What does the footprint change?","It overturns Crusoe's belief that the island is an entirely private and controllable space.",521,"The narration includes slavery and colonial hierarchies; Friday should not be reduced to a nameless helper.")
    A("Moll Flanders","Daniel Defoe",1722,"restoration","Novel","England and colonial Virginia","A woman's pursuit of security crosses the border between survival and crime.","Moll=Retrospective narrator seeking security|The governess=Facilitates theft and later helps Moll|The Lancashire husband=Partner reunited in prison", '''
A precarious beginning~Born to a woman imprisoned at Newgate, Moll grows up without reliable protection. Her desire to become a gentlewoman is inseparable from fear of dependence and poverty.~Moll~home~SECURITY becomes ambition
Marriage as opportunity~Moll's relationships and marriages promise advancement but repeatedly disappoint. Her retrospective narration asks readers to see financial calculation beside desire, deception and social vulnerability.~Moll~coins~LOVE meets survival
A terrible discovery~In Virginia, Moll discovers that her husband is her half-brother. The revelation makes apparently respectable domestic life another unstable foundation, and she eventually returns to England.~Moll~letter~RESPECTABILITY hides a shock
The Lancashire match~A marriage joins two people who have misrepresented their fortunes. Moll and her husband separate when the hoped-for financial security proves illusory, exposing deception on both sides.~Moll,The Lancashire husband~contract~TWO pretences meet
The criminal profession~With legitimate resources shrinking, Moll becomes an accomplished thief. The governess assists her, and success temporarily masks how quickly a mistake can turn survival into imprisonment.~Moll,The governess~mask~SKILL without safety
Newgate and repentance~Arrest brings Moll back to the prison associated with her birth. Facing death, she professes repentance and reunites with the Lancashire husband, though her self-presentation invites scrutiny.~Moll,The Lancashire husband~chains~THE BEGINNING returns
A colonial second chance~Transportation to Virginia leads toward wealth and a later return to England. The ending blends penitence and prosperity, leaving moral renewal entangled with economic reward.~Moll,The Lancashire husband~ship~REPENTANCE and profit''',"Why does money matter so much to Moll's choices?","Her insecure social position makes economic protection a persistent condition of independence and survival.",370,"A retrospective and potentially self-justifying narration; the full sequence of marriages is condensed.")
    A("Gulliver’s Travels","Jonathan Swift",1726,"restoration","Satirical travel fiction","Four imagined lands and England","Changing scale makes human pride look absurd.","Gulliver=Traveller and unreliable observer|The Lilliputians=Tiny people with familiar rivalries|The Brobdingnag king=Questions European civilisation|The Houyhnhnms=Rational horses|The Yahoos=Degraded humanlike beings", '''
Small people great quarrels~Shipwreck takes Gulliver to Lilliput, where tiny inhabitants bind his enormous body. Court politics and conflicts over trivial differences make disproportionate human pride visible.~Gulliver,The Lilliputians~rope~SMALL scale familiar vanity
A dangerous favourite~Gulliver serves the emperor but loses favour when he refuses complete domination of a rival country. Court suspicion converts yesterday's useful giant into today's enemy.~Gulliver,The Lilliputians~crown~SERVICE does not ensure trust
The giant kingdom~In Brobdingnag Gulliver becomes small and vulnerable. Everyday bodies and creatures seem immense, reversing the confidence he enjoyed when physical scale favoured him.~Gulliver,The Brobdingnag king~mirror~PERSPECTIVE reverses power
Europe under examination~Gulliver proudly explains European government and warfare. The giant king responds with disgust, making the traveller's patriotic account expose the cruelty it was meant to celebrate.~Gulliver,The Brobdingnag king~sword~PRIDE becomes evidence against itself
Ideas detached from life~Laputa and the academy at Lagado display learning divorced from practical needs. Failed experiments and abstract speculation parody knowledge that neglects the people it claims to help.~Gulliver~laboratory~REASON can lose contact
Horses and human shame~Among the Houyhnhnms, Gulliver admires rational order and identifies humans with the repulsive Yahoos. His admiration grows into a troubling rejection of his own species.~Gulliver,The Houyhnhnms,The Yahoos~village~IDEALISING creates estrangement
Home becomes unendurable~Back in England, Gulliver struggles to tolerate his family and prefers horses. The ending makes readers question not only human pride but the traveller's extreme response to it.~Gulliver,The Houyhnhnms~home~SATIRE also questions its narrator''',"Why is Gulliver's changing size useful to the satire?","It makes power and importance look relative, forcing familiar human assumptions into unfamiliar proportions.",829,"Four voyages are summarised; Gulliver's opinions do not automatically equal Swift's.")
    A("Tom Jones","Henry Fielding",1749,"restoration","Comic novel","Somerset and London","A good heart makes mistakes before it finds its place.","Tom Jones=Foundling with generous impulses|Sophia Western=Woman he loves|Squire Allworthy=Tom's guardian|Blifil=Respectable-looking rival|Partridge=Companion on the road", '''
A foundling is welcomed~Allworthy discovers an infant in his bed and raises him. Tom's obscure origins become a social disadvantage, while the household's legitimate child Blifil enjoys an appearance of respectability.~Tom Jones,Squire Allworthy,Blifil~home~BIRTH does not reveal character
Generosity without discipline~Tom's kindness coexists with impulsive behaviour. Sophia loves him, but class expectations and his sexual entanglements complicate the difference between a generous nature and responsible conduct.~Tom Jones,Sophia Western~heart~GOODNESS needs judgement
The rival controls the story~Blifil manipulates appearances and reports to damage Tom. Allworthy believes the accusations and sends Tom away, showing how moral authority can be misled by a polished exterior.~Tom Jones,Blifil,Squire Allworthy~letter~REPUTATION is constructed
The road becomes a school~Tom travels with Partridge through inns, accidents and encounters. The comic journey exposes varied motives and social ranks while delaying the simple reunion he desires.~Tom Jones,Partridge~road~EXPERIENCE complicates certainty
London and the compromised choice~Tom enters a world of patronage and sexual bargaining, including Lady Bellaston. Meanwhile Sophia resists being traded through marriage, preserving her right to judge him for herself.~Tom Jones,Sophia Western~coins~ADVANCEMENT has a cost
The truth of origins~A series of disclosures exposes Blifil's scheming and identifies Tom as Allworthy's nephew. Recognition restores his social position, but it does not erase Sophia's reasons for caution.~Tom Jones,Squire Allworthy,Blifil~book~TRUTH defeats the polished lie
A comic settlement~Tom demonstrates greater maturity, and Sophia accepts him. The ending joins inheritance and marriage with the novel's insistence that virtue requires attention to conduct rather than mere reputation.~Tom Jones,Sophia Western~garden~THE HEART learns restraint''',"How does Blifil contrast with Tom?","Blifil cultivates respectable appearances while acting selfishly; Tom is flawed but often generous.",6593)
    A("Tristram Shandy","Laurence Sterne",1759,"restoration","Experimental comic novel","The Shandy household and remembered journeys","A life story keeps escaping the straight line.","Tristram=Digressive narrator|Walter Shandy=Father with elaborate theories|Uncle Toby=Gentle veteran absorbed in fortifications|Trim=Toby's loyal companion|Widow Wadman=Interested in Toby", '''
Before the narrator is born~Tristram starts with his conception rather than his adult life. Family interruptions immediately turn a supposedly straightforward biography into a maze of explanations, anecdotes and postponed beginnings.~Tristram,Walter Shandy~clock~THE START keeps moving
Theory meets accident~Walter invests great hopes in systems governing names, education and bodies. Obstetrical mishaps and ordinary contingency repeatedly defeat the certainty of his carefully constructed theories.~Walter Shandy,Tristram~laboratory~LIFE resists a system
A name goes wrong~The child receives the name Tristram against Walter's wishes. The narrator turns this accident into an extended reflection on how language and expectation influence the story people tell about a life.~Tristram,Walter Shandy~letter~A NAME becomes a digression
Toby's small battlefields~Wounded in war, Uncle Toby reconstructs military sieges with Trim. Their hobby-horse provides comic absorption while revealing Toby's gentleness, loyalty and capacity for fellow-feeling.~Uncle Toby,Trim~castle~OBSESSION can reveal tenderness
Courtship and misunderstanding~Widow Wadman investigates the implications of Toby's wound, while he understands questions through his military interests. Their crossed intentions make language itself a comic source of confusion.~Uncle Toby,Widow Wadman~heart~WORDS follow different maps
The page refuses convention~Blank, black and marbled pages, interruptions and addresses to readers disrupt the physical form of the book. Reading becomes participation in the narrator's resistance to orderly representation.~Tristram~book~THE BOOK performs its argument
A life left unfinished~Tristram cannot keep pace with the events he wants to describe, and the work ends without completing his biography. The digressions become the substance rather than obstacles to a supposedly proper plot.~Tristram,Uncle Toby~road~THE DETOUR is the experience''',"Why does Tristram struggle to finish telling his life?","Digressions and the ongoing passage of time make complete, orderly narration impossible.",1079,"Published in nine volumes from 1759 to 1767. These frames follow major narrative devices, not a linear plot.",date="1759–1767")
    A("Songs of Innocence and of Experience","William Blake",1794,"romantic","Paired poetry collections","Children, streets and symbolic landscapes","Two ways of seeing expose the cost of lost innocence.","The child=Voice of trust and vulnerability|The piper=Poetic guide in Innocence|The bard=Challenging guide in Experience|The chimney sweeper=Child labourer|The speaker in London=Witness to oppression", '''
The piper's invitation~The introduction to Innocence imagines music becoming a book for children. Its apparent simplicity offers an imaginative world of tenderness, but that world is never wholly insulated from suffering.~The piper,The child~book~A CHILD'S view opens poetry
The lamb and the child~The Lamb joins gentleness, childhood and Christian imagery. Repeated questions become a way of recognising kinship between the speaker, the creature and a loving creator.~The child~garden~INNOCENCE sees connection
The labouring child~The chimney-sweeper poems place childhood beside dangerous work. A reassuring dream in Innocence and a sharper accusation in Experience reveal different ways of responding to the same injustice.~The chimney sweeper,The child~chains~COMFORT can conceal harm
The bard calls us awake~Experience replaces the piper's invitation with an urgent prophetic voice. Knowledge of constraint, fear and repression makes the reader reconsider what earlier trust may have overlooked.~The bard~storm~EXPERIENCE challenges sleep
The tiger's question~The Tyger asks how terrifying strength relates to creation. It does not settle the mystery, putting fierce energy beside the gentleness associated with the lamb.~The bard~fire~CREATION contains opposites
The city bears its marks~London's speaker hears and sees suffering shaped by institutions and inward restrictions. Streets become a map of power acting upon bodies, relationships and imagination.~The speaker in London~city~OPPRESSION enters the mind
Read the pairs together~The two collections stage contrary states rather than a simple ranking of innocence below experience. Their dialogue asks how vision might preserve tenderness while confronting injustice honestly.~The piper,The bard,The child~mirror~TWO visions test each other''',"Why should the paired poems be read together?","Their different perspectives expose both the value of innocence and the suffering or repression that innocence may miss.",1934,"Innocence appeared in 1789; the combined volume in 1794. Symbolic voices recur across separate poems.",date="1789; combined 1794")
    A("Tintern Abbey","William Wordsworth",1798,"romantic","Meditative poem","The Wye valley on a return visit","A landscape remembered becomes a resource for living.","The speaker=Returns after five years|Dorothy=His sister and companion", '''
The place is seen again~After five years the speaker revisits the banks of the Wye. Cliffs, trees and signs of rural life begin an encounter between a present landscape and its remembered earlier form.~The speaker,Dorothy~river~RETURN activates memory
The absent landscape worked~During years away, recollections offered calm amid urban weariness. Nature matters not only when directly seen but when memory quietly alters a person's emotional and moral life.~The speaker~city~MEMORY travels with us
The earlier self returns~The speaker recalls his youthful response to nature as an intense physical delight. Revisiting the place makes him aware that his present mind cannot simply recover that earlier mode.~The speaker~forest~THE SELF has changed
A deeper kind of attention~Maturity brings awareness of human suffering and a more reflective sense of connection. The apparent loss of youthful excitement is accompanied by another way of perceiving the world.~The speaker~mirror~LOSS makes room for insight
Dorothy carries the old energy~Watching his sister, the speaker recognises something of his former self. Her presence turns private recollection into a relationship between different stages of experience.~The speaker,Dorothy~heart~ANOTHER person holds a memory
A wish for future comfort~He hopes Dorothy will remember the landscape and their shared visit when she faces loneliness or fear. The poem imagines remembrance as protection rather than as escape from every difficulty.~The speaker,Dorothy~garden~TODAY may sustain tomorrow
A shared place~The ending joins love for the landscape to love for his sister. Its lasting value now includes the knowledge that they stood there together and may preserve the experience differently.~The speaker,Dorothy~river~PLACE becomes relationship''',"How does nature help even when the speaker is away from it?","Remembered landscapes bring calm and shape his feelings and moral attention.", "https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poems/45527/lines-composed-a-few-miles-above-tintern-abbey-on-revisiting-the-banks-of-the-wye-during-a-tour-july-13-1798","Full title begins Lines Composed a Few Miles above Tintern Abbey. A reflective progression; the abbey itself is not the central described object.")
    A("The Prelude","William Wordsworth",1850,"romantic","Autobiographical blank-verse poem","Childhood landscapes, Britain and revolutionary France","The growing mind discovers how it becomes a poet.","The poetic self=Recollects the growth of imagination|Coleridge=Friend and principal addressee|Dorothy=Supportive sister|Beaupuy=French republican acquaintance", '''
A mind turns backward~The narrator looks toward a poetic vocation by examining the formation of his own mind. Childhood becomes an active source of imaginative growth, rather than merely a collection of charming memories.~The poetic self,Coleridge~book~THE SUBJECT is a growing mind
The stolen boat~A furtive rowing excursion changes from pleasure to fear when a mountain seems to loom after the boy. Nature unsettles mastery and continues to work upon his imagination after the event.~The poetic self~mountain~FEAR enlarges imagination
Learning among landscapes~School, skating and solitary exploration develop the senses and emotions. Pleasure and disturbance together teach the young mind to respond to powers it cannot wholly control.~The poetic self~forest~NATURE teaches through feeling
Books and crowded life~Cambridge and London widen experience beyond the childhood landscape. Learning, theatre and crowds challenge the narrator to recognise imagination amid intellectual systems and urban distraction.~The poetic self~city~THE WORLD becomes more complex
Revolution promises renewal~Travel in France and friendship with Beaupuy encourage hopes of liberty and social transformation. Political commitment connects imaginative aspiration with the lives and suffering of others.~The poetic self,Beaupuy~road~HOPE enters history
Disillusion and recovery~Violence and political disappointment shake the narrator's confidence. Nature, affection and Dorothy's companionship help restore a capacity for feeling and thought beyond abstract systems.~The poetic self,Dorothy~storm~CARE restores imagination
A vocation takes shape~Later visionary experiences, including Snowdon, support the narrator's understanding of poetic power. Addressing Coleridge, he presents the developed imagination as an ongoing responsibility rather than a finished achievement.~The poetic self,Coleridge~mountain~IMAGINATION becomes vocation''',"Why is the boat-stealing episode important?","Fear transforms an ordinary excursion into an experience that continues to shape the child's imagination.", "https://viscomi.sites.oasis.unc.edu/viscomi/coursepack/wordsworth/Wordsworth-Prelude.pdf","Published posthumously in 1850, with earlier major versions in 1799 and 1805. Placed with Romantic works by movement despite its Victorian publication date.")
    A("The Rime of the Ancient Mariner","S. T. Coleridge",1798,"romantic","Supernatural narrative poem","A wedding frame and a haunted sea voyage","A senseless killing becomes a lifelong duty to remember.","The Mariner=Compelled storyteller|The Wedding Guest=Reluctant listener|The crew=Shipmates sharing the voyage|Life-in-Death=Supernatural winner of the Mariner", '''
The wedding interrupted~An old sailor stops a Wedding Guest and compels him to listen. A cheerful social occasion becomes the frame for a story that will change the listener's understanding of life.~The Mariner,The Wedding Guest~gate~A STORY demands attention
The bird follows the ship~After ice and danger, an albatross appears and accompanies the sailors. The Mariner kills it with his crossbow without supplying a convincing reason, breaking a relationship of apparent hospitality.~The Mariner,The crew~ice~A NEEDLESS act begins guilt
A still and thirsty sea~Wind fails and the sailors suffer surrounded by undrinkable water. They hang the dead bird around the Mariner's neck, turning an action into a visible burden of blame.~The Mariner,The crew~sea~GUILT becomes a weight
The spectral game~Death and Life-in-Death gamble aboard a ghostly ship. The crew die, while the Mariner remains alive among their accusing eyes, condemned to a more isolating form of suffering.~The Mariner,Life-in-Death,The crew~ghost~SURVIVAL can be punishment
An unplanned blessing~He sees the water snakes as beautiful and blesses them without calculating a reward. The albatross falls from his neck, marking a change from contempt toward living things to love.~The Mariner~sea~LOVE begins release
The return and the confession~Supernatural forces move the ship home, where it sinks. The Mariner seeks the Hermit's absolution and discovers that telling his story relieves a recurring agony.~The Mariner~ship~CONFESSION must return
A sadder wiser listener~The sailor continues to travel and retell his lesson about reverence for life. The Wedding Guest turns away from the celebration, altered by the experience of hearing rather than living the voyage.~The Mariner,The Wedding Guest~book~REMEMBER all living things''',"What releases the albatross from the Mariner's neck?","His spontaneous blessing of the water snakes, a moment of uncalculated love for living things.",151,"First version appeared in Lyrical Ballads. This individual entry expands the poem only briefly introduced in the collection.")
    A("Kubla Khan","S. T. Coleridge",1816,"romantic","Visionary fragment","Xanadu and a remembered imaginative vision","A pleasure palace rises beside forces no ruler can contain.","Kubla=Ruler ordering the pleasure dome|The visionary speaker=Imagines recovering creative power|The Abyssinian maid=Figure whose music the speaker remembers", '''
The dome is decreed~Kubla commands a pleasure dome in Xanadu. Ordered gardens and enclosing walls initially suggest human power shaping a magnificent and protected space.~Kubla~castle~COMMAND designs a world
The river travels beyond order~Alph flows through vast caverns toward a sunless sea. The landscape immediately exceeds the ruler's gardens, connecting controlled beauty to depths that cannot be surveyed.~Kubla~river~ORDER has an outside
The chasm erupts~A violent fountain bursts from a wild cleft, hurling fragments through the air. Creative energy appears at once fertile, sacred and frightening, resisting a merely decorative reading of the scene.~Kubla~mountain~CREATION is turbulent
The ancestors warn~Kubla hears voices prophesying war while river and dome coexist in the visionary landscape. Even the pleasure of the palace is shadowed by conflict and instability.~Kubla~storm~DELIGHT is not secure
Music returns in memory~The speaker recalls an Abyssinian maid playing a dulcimer. Her song suggests a different source of creation, moving the poem from an imperial command toward inward imaginative recovery.~The visionary speaker,The Abyssinian maid~book~MEMORY offers a song
The wished-for reconstruction~If he could revive the music within himself, the speaker imagines building the dome through poetry. The palace becomes an image of artistic power rather than simply a historical structure.~The visionary speaker~castle~POETRY rebuilds the vision
The poet becomes uncanny~An audience would respond to such a creator with awe and protective ritual. The fragment leaves imaginative power both desirable and disturbing, and its promised reconstruction remains conditional.~The visionary speaker~stars~INSPIRATION is powerful and elusive''',"What changes when the Abyssinian maid appears?","The focus shifts from Kubla's commanded palace to the speaker's wish to recover a musical vision and create through poetry.",11101,"Published 1816. Coleridge's preface describes a dream and an interruption; these are part of his account, not independently verified facts.")
